### SpecForge — connect mdasberg/requirements, then browse what it imported
#
# Run against the composed stack: `docker compose up -d --wait` (PostgreSQL on 30301, Keycloak on
# 8081) and `./gradlew bootRun` on 8080. Pick the `dev` environment in the client before sending
# anything — the repository, branch, glob and project live in `http-client.env.json`, so this file
# connects a different repository by editing one place.
#
# The requests are in order and chained: each stores what the next needs (`token`,
# `installationId`, `scanId`, `connectionId`, `specId`), so run them top to bottom the first time.
# Afterwards any single request can be re-sent on its own.
#
# Step 1e registers the installation the connection flow needs. Steps 2 to 5 are that flow,
# which has no UI yet. Steps 6 onward are the catalogue.
#
# https://github.com/mdasberg/requirements is private and holds 21 specifications at
# `openspecs/specs/<clm|fin|mem>/<capability>/spec.md`. Two things about that layout matter:
#
#   1. The directory is `openspecs`, with an s, and the files sit two levels deep. The OpenSpec
#      default glob `openspec/specs/**/spec.md` matches NONE of them. The glob in the environment
#      is `openspecs/specs/**/spec.md`.
#   2. Private means the GitHub App has to be installed on it explicitly — Only select
#      repositories. Until it is, request 2 lists no installation to connect through.
#
# The domain a specification lands in is the segment under `specs`, so these import as `clm`, `fin`
# and `mem` — the three the repository is already organised around.

### 1. Sign in
# Keycloak is the only user store — SpecForge issues no credentials of its own. The dev realm
# enables the direct access grant so a token can be had without a browser; a real realm does not.
# The realm ships three users, each with the roles their name implies:
#   reviewer / reviewer        REVIEWER
#   architect / architect      REVIEWER, ARCHITECT
#   admin-user / admin-user    REVIEWER, ADMIN   ← connecting a repository needs this one
POST {{keycloak}}/realms/specforge/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id=specforge-web&username={{user}}&password={{password}}

> {%
    client.global.set("token", response.body.access_token);
    client.test("a token came back", () => client.assert(response.status === 200));
%}

### 1b. Who am I
# The local mirror of the token: display name, actor kind and the realm roles. Requests 3 and 4
# need ADMIN, so this asserts it rather than letting them fail with a 403 that does not say which
# token was sent.
GET {{host}}/api/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    client.test("the current token can connect a repository", () => {
        const roles = response.body.roles ?? [];
        client.assert(roles.includes("ADMIN"),
            "The token in `token` is " + (response.body.name ?? "unknown") + " with roles ["
            + roles.join(", ") + "], and requests 3 and 4 need ADMIN. Re-send request 1 as "
            + "admin-user; a service-account token from request 1c holds no realm role at all.");
    });
%}

### 1c. A check runner's token
# Agents authenticate through the confidential client's service account, not as a person. Its
# `actorKind` is AGENT — the field that keeps human and automated output distinguishable. This
# stores `agentToken` and deliberately leaves `token` alone: a service account holds no realm role,
# so overwriting `token` here would make requests 3 and 4 fail with 403 "This role is not permitted
# to perform that action" several steps later. Swap the header below to {{agentToken}} on the one
# request you want to send as an agent.
POST {{keycloak}}/realms/specforge/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=specforge-api&client_secret=specforge-api-dev-secret

> {% client.global.set("agentToken", response.body.access_token); %}

### 1d. Who the agent is
# `actorKind` is AGENT and `roles` is `["REVIEWER"]` — every identity is at least a reviewer, agent
# or not (Identity.yaml: roles is never empty). What keeps an agent from acting as a person is that
# it never holds ARCHITECT or ADMIN: no service account has them, so it can comment but never
# approve (the approval rule requires ARCHITECT) and never connect a repository (ADMIN-only).
GET {{host}}/api/me
Authorization: Bearer {{agentToken}}

### 1e. Register the GitHub App installation
# An installation is only ever created by the `installation` webhook — no endpoint creates one — so
# a local instance with nothing tunnelled into it shows an empty list at request 2 forever. This
# posts the delivery GitHub would have sent, signed with the same secret the running instance was
# given, so getting started needs no tunnel and no webhook URL on the app at all.
#
# `webhookSecret` and `installationExternalId` live in `http-client.private.env.json`, which is
# gitignored: the secret is a credential and the installation id is yours. Take the id from
# https://github.com/settings/installations → Configure on the app — it is the last segment of that
# page's URL.
#
# The signature is computed over the body as it is actually sent, so editing the body needs no
# other change. The delivery id is a fresh UUID every send: a repeated delivery id is deliberately
# acknowledged without being acted on, which would make a re-send look like a silent failure.
#
# The posted account is not read. The handler asks GitHub what the installation grants, so the
# repositories request 2 lists come from GitHub and never from this payload — which also means
# `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_APP_ID` and `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY` must already be set on the running
# instance, or this is accepted and registers nothing.
< {%
    const secret = request.environment.get("webhookSecret");
    const installation = request.environment.get("installationExternalId");
    if (!secret || !/^\d+$/.test(installation ?? "")) {
        throw new Error("`webhookSecret` and `installationExternalId` come from "
            + "http/http-client.private.env.json, which is gitignored and may not exist yet — see "
            + "the README. The installation id must be the bare number, since it is posted as JSON.");
    }
    request.variables.set("accountLogin", request.environment.get("repositoryFullName").split("/")[0]);
    request.variables.set("signature", crypto.hmac.sha256()
        .withTextSecret(secret)
        .updateWithText(request.body.tryGetSubstituted())
        .digest()
        .toHex());
%}
POST {{host}}/api/webhooks/github
X-GitHub-Event: installation
X-GitHub-Delivery: {{$uuid}}
X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256={{signature}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"action":"created","installation":{"id":{{installationExternalId}},"account":{"login":"{{accountLogin}}","type":"User"}}}

> {%
    client.test("the delivery was accepted", () => {
        client.assert(response.status === 202,
            "401 means the signature did not verify: `webhookSecret` is not the "
            + "SPECFORGE_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET the running instance was given.");
    });
%}

### 2. The forge installations SpecForge can read through
# Nothing here creates an installation. It arrives over the GitHub App's `installation` webhook
# (request 1e), and the repositories it grants are the boundary every read stays inside. An empty
# list means the app is not installed on the repository, or `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_APP_ID` /
# `SPECFORGE_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY` are unset — without them the forge client refuses every call
# rather than pretending a repository is unreachable. See the README for the app's setup.
GET {{host}}/api/forge/installations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("installationId", first.id);
    }
    // Assert here rather than letting requests 3 to 5 fail on an unresolved `{{installationId}}`
    // two steps later, which reports a missing path variable instead of the actual cause.
    client.test("an installation exists to connect through", () => {
        client.assert(first !== undefined,
            "No forge installation. Send request 1e, which posts the `installation` delivery "
            + "this instance never received; a 202 there and still nothing here means the app is "
            + "not installed on the repository, or the instance has no app id and private key. "
            + "Requests 3 to 5 cannot succeed until this list is non-empty.");
    });
%}

### 3. Scan before connecting                                                          [ADMIN]
# What would import, what is a change proposal rather than a specification, and what cannot be
# parsed — answered before anything is created. Returns 202 with a PENDING scan; it runs
# asynchronously.
POST {{host}}/api/forge/scans
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "installationId": "{{installationId}}",
  "repositoryFullName": "{{repositoryFullName}}",
  "branch": "{{branch}}",
  "pathGlob": "{{pathGlob}}",
  "specFormat": "{{specFormat}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("scanId", response.body.id);
    client.test("the scan was accepted", () => {
        client.assert(response.status === 202,
            "The scan was not started, so requests 3b and 4 have no scan to refer to. A 404 here "
            + "means `installationId` is unset or unknown; re-run request 2.");
    });
%}

### 3b. Read the scan
# Re-send until the status settles. For this repository, expect 21 importable and nothing
# unparsable: every file carries a heading, and there is no `changes` directory, so nothing is
# classified as a change proposal. `files` carries a row per matched path with why it was
# classified as it was, so "why is this file not a specification" is answerable without scanning
# again.
#
# A glob that matches nothing shows here as zero of everything — which is the cheap way to find out
# before request 4 refuses the connection.
#
# With no GitHub App configured the scan FAILS rather than returning nothing, and says so:
#
#   "status": "FAILED",
#   "failureReason": "No GitHub App is configured; set specforge.github.app-id and private-key."
#
# Note that a scan is not a grant check — it will attempt any repository name. The grant is
# enforced when the connection is created.
GET {{host}}/api/forge/scans/{{scanId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 4. Connect the repository                                                          [ADMIN]
# Creates the connection and triggers the initial import in one call. Three things are refused
# here: a repository the installation does not grant, a repository, branch and path already
# connected (409, never a silent duplicate), and a glob that matches nothing importable (422 — a
# connection that imports nothing is a dead end).
#
# `syncMode` is ON_PULL_REQUEST: a pull request touching a spec is the moment a team wants review,
# and approvals report back as a commit status. ON_PUSH and MANUAL are the alternatives.
#
# The approval rule is stored verbatim and never read by this module; the approval capability
# evaluates it.
POST {{host}}/api/repository-connections
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "installationId": "{{installationId}}",
  "repositoryFullName": "{{repositoryFullName}}",
  "branch": "{{branch}}",
  "pathGlob": "{{pathGlob}}",
  "specFormat": "{{specFormat}}",
  "syncMode": "{{syncMode}}",
  "scanId": "{{scanId}}",
  "project": {
    "name": "{{projectName}}",
    "team": "{{projectTeam}}",
    "tracker": "NONE",
    "approvalRule": {
      "minApprovals": 2,
      "requiredRoles": ["ARCHITECT"]
    }
  }
}

> {%
    client.global.set("connectionId", response.body.id);
    client.test("the repository was connected", () => {
        client.assert(response.status === 201,
            "Not connected. 404: the installation does not grant this repository — a scan will "
            + "attempt any name, but connecting checks the grant. 409: this repository, branch and "
            + "path is already connected. 422: the glob matched nothing importable — check "
            + "request 3b first.");
    });
%}

### 4b. The connections the caller can see
# A connection whose installation was revoked or suspended shows as DEGRADED with a reason, not
# deleted: losing a grant is not a reason to lose the review history of what the repository held.
GET {{host}}/api/repository-connections
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("connectionId", first.id);
    }
%}

### 5. Watch the import
# A row per file including the ones nothing happened to. `importedCount` counts new versions,
# `unchangedCount` the files whose content hash matched what was already stored — re-importing an
# unchanged file from a later commit is deliberately a no-op.
GET {{host}}/api/repository-connections/{{connectionId}}/import-runs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 5b. Re-import by hand                                                              [ADMIN]
# Independent of the sync mode. Useful after pushing to the branch when sync is ON_PULL_REQUEST.
POST {{host}}/api/repository-connections/{{connectionId}}/imports
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6. Browse the catalogue
# Grouped by project unless `groupBy` says otherwise. The group counts are computed over the whole
# filtered result rather than over the returned page, so they still sum to `total`.
GET {{host}}/api/specs
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    const first = response.body.items[0];
    if (first) {
        client.global.set("specId", first.id);
    }
    client.test("the catalogue holds something to read", () => {
        client.assert(first !== undefined,
            "The catalogue is empty, so requests 7, 7b and 9 have no specification to address. "
            + "Connect a repository first (requests 2 to 4) and watch the import in request 5.");
    });
%}

### 6b. Group by domain
# For this repository: clm, fin and mem. The domain is the segment under `specs`, so a repository
# that nests one level deeper still groups the way it is organised rather than reporting every
# capability as its own domain.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?groupBy=DOMAIN
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6c. Group by repository
# PROJECT, REPOSITORY, DOMAIN or TEAM. The same specifications, bucketed differently.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?groupBy=REPOSITORY
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6d. Filters combine
# Every filter is optional, they apply as a conjunction, and a repeated parameter matches any of
# its values. This asks for specifications that are (in review OR changes requested) AND in the
# claims domain.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?status=IN_REVIEW&status=CHANGES_REQUESTED&domain=clm
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6e. Search
# Titles, repository paths, section headings and body text. Each hit names the section that matched
# and returns a snippet with the terms wrapped in `[[` and `]]` — deliberately not HTML, because
# the snippet is body text from someone else's repository. A heading match outranks a body match.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?q=adjudication
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6f. Search applies on top of the filters, not instead of them
GET {{host}}/api/specs?q=remittance&domain=fin&groupBy=TEAM
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 6g. Page through
# `cursor` comes from the previous response and is absent on the last page. It is validated on the
# way in — it arrives in a URL, and a URL is edited.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?limit=5
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

> {%
    // Always set it, even when there is no next page. Leaving it unset sends a literal
    // `{{cursor}}` in request 6h, which the API rejects with
    // "'{{cursor}}' is not a cursor from a previous response." — a confusing way to be told
    // there was only one page. An empty cursor is simply the first page again.
    client.global.set("cursor", response.body.cursor ?? "");
%}

### 6h. The next page
# With fewer than five specifications there is no next page, the cursor is empty, and this returns
# the first page again rather than failing.
GET {{host}}/api/specs?limit=5&cursor={{cursor}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 7. Read a specification
# The document's metadata, the current version's markdown body and outline, and every version that
# can be selected — one response, so opening a document is one request rather than three. The body
# is markdown; SpecForge never emits HTML.
GET {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 7b. Read an older version
# `current` is false and the version selector still lists all of them. An ordinal that does not
# exist is a 404.
GET {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}?version=1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 8. The projects overview
# Per project: its connected repositories, counts per lifecycle status, and open reviews. Built
# from the imported specifications themselves, so a project appears once it holds one.
GET {{host}}/api/projects
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 9. Editing a specification is refused — always 409
# The connected repository is the source of truth and SpecForge is a read-only mirror. The problem
# document names the repository and path, directing the author to change the file in git. This
# endpoint exists to say so in the contract rather than to leave the rule implicit.
PUT {{host}}/api/specs/{{specId}}/content
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": "# Claim Specification\n\nEdited in SpecForge, which is not a thing.\n"
}

### 10. The contract itself
# The bundled document the server generates from `src/main/resources/openapi/`, not something a
# scanner reconstructed from annotations.
GET {{host}}/api/openapi.json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 11. No token at all — 401
# Every endpoint is authenticated; there is no anonymous read.
GET {{host}}/api/specs
